# **Section 1: The Digital Learning Pyramid**

The Digital Learning Pyramid is the strategic architecture through which the Digital Pedagogy Coach role operates across Weston College. It has three levels, each representing a distinct phase of digital capability development for staff and learners. Everything in this document, from skills to tools to CPD routes, is organised in relation to these three levels.

The Pyramid is built bottom-up. Digital Foundations must come first. They are the condition for everything else. Digital Inclusion builds on those foundations, extending good practice into accessible, equitable environments for all learners. Digital Innovation is the highest level, where individual practice influences systems, shapes strategy, and challenges what is possible.

| **Why a Pyramid?** A pyramid communicates sequence and dependency. You cannot build inclusion without foundations. You cannot innovate sustainably without inclusion. The three levels are not separate programmes. They are a journey. Every member of staff is somewhere on that journey. This document is designed to help them know where they are, what comes next, and how to get there. |
| --- |

*The table below maps the three levels of the Pyramid to their definition, the staff development stage they correspond to, and the primary strategic focus at each level. The Pyramid logo will be embedded in the HTML resource as a visual anchor for this framework throughout the toolkit.*

| **Level 1 — The Base** **Digital Foundations** *Including Digital Accessibility* | Every member of staff can use the tools available to them with confidence. Devices, software, email, digital learning environments, digital resourcing, and cloud storage are used reliably and professionally. Accessibility is understood as a professional standard, not an add-on. Every digital resource created by a staff member passes the Office Accessibility Checker before it is shared. WCAG 2.2 AA is the floor. Immersive Reader, Live Captions, and the Accessibility Checker are part of everyday practice. |
| --- | --- |

| **Level 2 — The Middle** **Digital Inclusion** *Developing Practice for All Learners* | Staff use digital tools purposefully to improve teaching, learning, and assessment for all learners. They understand how to use assistive technology, design inclusive digital environments, and adapt their practice for ESOL, SEND, and disadvantaged learners. Teams environments are well structured, Assignments use rubrics, and learners receive accessible digital feedback. This level aligns with UDL and Quality First Teaching principles. Staff at this level have moved beyond personal competence into pedagogical application. |
| --- | --- |

| **Level 3 — The Pinnacle** **Digital Innovation** *Leading Change and Shaping Practice* | Staff at this level are not just using digital tools. They are redesigning what teaching looks like with them. They innovate with AI, build automated workflows, challenge assumptions about what is possible, and influence others. They are Digital Leads, Digital Champions, and agents of change across the college. Their practice does not stay in their classroom. It spreads. |
| --- | --- |

## **How the Pyramid aligns to the Teacher Digital Skills stages**

| **Pyramid Level** | **ETF DTPF Stage** | **DigCompEdu** | **Weston College Focus** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Digital Foundations (Level 1) | Stage 1: Exploring | A1 to A2 — Newcomer to Explorer | Confident use of M365 tools, accessibility habits established, WCAG 2.2 AA as a professional standard |
| Digital Inclusion (Level 2a) | Stage 2A: Adopting, Developing | B1 — Integrator | Digital tools purposefully integrated into TLA; learner inclusion driving decisions; AT in use |
| Digital Inclusion (Level 2b) | Stage 2B: Adopting, Embedding | B2 — Expert | Accessibility by design; AI used critically and transparently; EDS mapping articulated; QFT and UDL embedded in digital practice |
| Digital Innovation (Level 3) | Stage 3: Leading | C1 to C2 — Leader to Pioneer | Coaching others; shaping strategy; building workflows; AI orchestration; driving institutional change |

| **The Learn, Work, Life alignment** The Digital Learning Pyramid connects directly to the college's three-context model for digital skills: Learn (how staff and learners develop digital capability for study and professional growth), Work (how digital skills translate into employment-ready practice in vocational and professional contexts), and Life (how inclusive digital practice enables every learner to participate fully in society, access services, and live independently). Every level of the Pyramid speaks to all three contexts. |
| --- |

# **Section 2: Top 10 Digital Skills Aligned to the Pyramid**

Each of the ten universal digital skills is placed within the Pyramid and mapped to the three-context model. This alignment supports coaching conversations with Heads of Area and helps staff understand why each skill matters and at which level of development it sits.

The context column uses the Learn, Work, Life language throughout, in line with college policy.

| **Rank** | **Skill** | **Pyramid Level** | **Learn, Work, Life** | **Why this level** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 01 | AI Literacy and Prompt Skills | Digital Inclusion (Level 2b) | Work; Life | Critical, active use of AI tools is not a foundational skill. It requires strong digital foundations and inclusive judgment before it can be used ethically and effectively. |
| 02 | Digital Communication and Collaboration | Digital Foundations (Level 1) | Learn; Work; Life | Professional use of Teams, email, and shared platforms is a baseline expectation for all staff and learners. The starting point, not a specialism. |
| 03 | Information and Data Handling | Digital Foundations to Inclusion (Level 1 to 2) | Learn; Work | Finding and evaluating information is foundational. Using it to inform decisions, map to EDS categories, or produce evidence moves into Inclusion. |
| 04 | Online Safety and Cybersecurity | Digital Foundations (Level 1) | Life; Work | Password hygiene, two-factor authentication, recognising phishing: these are non-negotiable baselines. Safety comes first. |
| 05 | Digital Productivity Tools | Digital Foundations (Level 1) | Learn; Work | Word, Excel, PowerPoint, OneNote, Teams, cloud storage: the tools of organised, professional digital practice. Must be in place before anything else can be built. It is essential that all digital learning environments and digital resourcing is accessible to all users and enables the use of assistive technology. |
| 06 | Problem Solving with Technology | Digital Inclusion (Level 2) | Work; Learn | Using digital tools to find solutions rather than seek step-by-step guidance signals a shift from user to practitioner. This is the gateway to inclusion. This can come from basic uses of AI, interpreting data, using and promoting the use of assistive technology. |
| 07 | Digital Transactions and Services | Digital Foundations (Level 1) | Life | Accessing government services, online banking, NHS App, Universal Credit portal: infrastructure for independent living. A social inclusion imperative. |
| 08 | Creating and Sharing Digital Content | Digital Inclusion (Level 2) | Learn; Work; Life | Creating accessible, purposeful digital content for defined audiences marks the shift from consuming to contributing. Accessibility by design is a requirement here. As is the promotion of Assistive Technology to support work, life, and learn. |
| 09 | Virtual Collaboration and Remote Working | Digital Inclusion (Level 2) | Work | Professional digital etiquette and effective hybrid working are not just personal competencies. They shape learner environments and inclusion. Strong use of Teams learning environments and accessible digital resources is a must. |
| 10 | Digital Wellbeing and Inclusion | Digital Inclusion (Level 2) | Life; Learn | Designing for accessibility, understanding tech's impact on wellbeing, and promoting inclusive digital environments: this is the heart of Learning Without Barriers. We have tools within the M365 ecosystem that support inclusion and wellbeing. |

# **Section 3: General Digital Skills Aligned to the Pyramid**

The five EDS categories applied across the three contexts of Learn, Work, and Life, each placed within the Digital Learning Pyramid. This section replaces the previous For Learning, For Work, For Society language throughout the framework, in line with college policy.

## **For Learn**

| **Skill** | **Pyramid Level** | **Context** | **What it looks like** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Navigating digital platforms | Foundations (Level 1) | Learn | Using Teams, SharePoint, and the Brighter Futures Hub (Moodle) VLE and other platforms to access timetables, resources, and learning materials independently without prompting |
| Research and information literacy | Foundations to Inclusion (Level 1 to 2) | Learn; Life | Specific search terms; evaluating source credibility; identifying AI generated misinformation |
| Digital note taking and organisation | Foundations (Level 1) | Learn | OneNote, Loops, other note-taking resources, and structured folders; version history; consistent naming conventions |
| Collaborative digital working | Inclusion (Level 2) | Learn; Work | Co-editing in real time; contributing to Teams channels; managing asynchronous peer review |
| AI assisted learning | Innovation (Level 3) | Learn; Work | Using AI tools to support research, drafting, and self quizzing with full academic integrity awareness. Active and critical use, not passive reliance. Utilising multiple forms of AI assistance to improve the quality of outcomes and reliable responses. |

## **For Work**

| **Skill** | **Pyramid Level** | **Context** | **What it looks like** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Professional communication | Foundations (Level 1) | Work | Email and Teams etiquette; video call protocol; knowing when to use which channel. NB — These are genuine skill gaps in most cohorts. |
| Productivity and office applications | Foundations (Level 1) | Work; Learn | Word, Excel, PowerPoint, cloud storage, Outlook calendar; understanding how applications combine to save time |
| Digital transactions and invoicing | Foundations (Level 1) | Work; Life | Online payments; Xero and Sage basics; HMRC Making Tax Digital; digital VAT submission. NB — Mandatory from 2026 for self-employed practitioners. |
| Data handling and record keeping | Inclusion (Level 2) | Work | Accurate digital data entry; GDPR obligations; compliant records; interpreting basic workplace data |
| AI literacy for the workplace | Innovation (Level 3) | Work | Using AI assistants to draft, summarise, and research; evaluating outputs critically; understanding ethical use and limitations |

## **For Life**

| **Skill** | **Pyramid Level** | **Context** | **What it looks like** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Accessing public and government services | Foundations (Level 1) | Life | Gov.UK, Universal Credit portal, NHS App, HMRC self-assessment. This is the infrastructure for independent living, not optional awareness. |
| Online safety and privacy | Foundations (Level 1) | Life; Work | Password managers; two-factor authentication; recognising phishing; reviewing social media privacy settings actively |
| Digital financial inclusion | Foundations (Level 1) | Life | Online banking; comparing providers; avoiding scams; understanding digital payment systems |
| Critical engagement with media | Inclusion (Level 2) | Life | Identifying misinformation; understanding algorithmic bias and filter bubbles; responsible social media use |
| Digital wellbeing and accessibility | Inclusion (Level 2) | Life; Learn | Screen time management; tech's impact on mental health; using and creating WCAG 2.2 AA accessible digital content; assistive technology awareness |

# **Section 4: Teacher Digital Skills Framework**

A fully expanded framework for FE and HE teaching staff at Weston College. Each stage sets out the skills staff should be demonstrating, the Microsoft and wider digital tools that anchor that stage, and the professional development levers from the Quality Handbook that support progression. The framework is designed to be useful for teaching staff navigating their own development, for TLAMs planning CPD, and for the Quality team evidencing digital capability growth across the college.

| **Two dimensions of Teacher Digital Skills** This framework covers two distinct but inseparable dimensions. General Digital Skills: the personal digital competencies that every staff member needs to work effectively in a digital organisation. Teaching Digital Skills: the pedagogical capabilities that translate personal competence into high quality, inclusive teaching, learning, and assessment. Both dimensions are present at every stage. The difference is in how they are applied and evidenced. |
| --- |

| **How to use this framework** This is not an audit tool. It is a development conversation tool. When a TLAM or coach sits down with a member of staff, the question is not: which boxes can you tick? The question is: where are you now, what does the next stage look like for you, and what support is available to help you get there? The Jisc Building Digital Capability Discovery Tool provides the baseline self-assessment. This framework provides the roadmap. |
| --- |

## **Stage 1: Exploring**

### **Pyramid Level: Digital Foundations | ETF DTPF: Exploring | DigCompEdu: A1 to A2**

Staff at Stage 1 are beginning to engage with digital tools in their professional practice. They may be new to the FE sector, new to Weston College, or new to thinking about digital as a dimension of their teaching. They are aware of what is available but are not yet acting on it consistently. Their focus should be on building confidence with the tools they need every day, and on establishing accessibility as a professional habit from the outset.

### **General Digital Skills — Stage 1**

| **Skill Area** | **What staff should be able to demonstrate** |
| --- | --- |
| Device and account management | Log into college email, Teams, and SharePoint reliably. Use OneDrive to save and retrieve files. Understand the difference between local and cloud storage. Know how to access IT support. |
| Email and communication | Write and send professional emails. Use appropriate tone. Reply with context. Attach files correctly. Use Teams chat for internal messaging. Know the difference between a reply and a reply all. |
| File management | Create folders, name files consistently, and retrieve them reliably. Avoid duplicate files. Understand version history. Share files via OneDrive link rather than email attachment. Understand and use file permissions correctly for different audiences. |
| Online safety basics | Use a strong, unique password for the college account. Enable two-factor authentication. Recognise and respond to phishing emails appropriately. Understand that personal and work data should not be mixed on college devices. Stay up to date with cyber and online safety training. |
| Accessing digital services | Navigate SharePoint and the Quality Hub. Know where to access digital support. Access the staff CPD menu. Stay up to date with basic digital pedagogy to support TLA practice. Complete the New Staff Induction digital onboarding tasks. Use Gov.UK and DfE digital services relevant to role. |

### **Teaching Digital Skills — Stage 1**

| **Skill Area** | **What staff should be able to demonstrate** |
| --- | --- |
| Setting up a class Team | Create a class Team using the Education template. Add a welcoming first channel. Organise Channels to reflect the Scheme of Learning or planned learning experience. Upload a scheme of learning and key resources — these must be accessible by design. Invite students. Post a welcome message. Know how to navigate channels and the Files tab. |
| Using Assignments | Create a basic assignment in Teams with a due date and attached brief. Know how to view, mark, feedback, and return student work. Understand the difference between Grades and Class Notebook. |
| Basic accessibility | Understand the responsibilities we have for providing digitally accessible content. Run the Microsoft Office Accessibility Checker on any document before uploading it to Teams. Understand what alt text is and add it to images. Use built-in heading styles in Word rather than manual bold text. |
| Attendance and record keeping | Record attendance digitally in the college system. Keep basic digital records of learner progress — this includes collecting baseline data. Know where lesson documentation should be saved. |
| Awareness of inclusive tools | Know that Immersive Reader exists and what it does. Demonstrate it to all learners at induction, and regularly after, as a standard offer, not only those with a recorded need. Know what assistive technology is available to learners, and promote its use to all learners, not just those with a recorded need. Know that Live Captions can be enabled in Teams meetings. |

### **Microsoft Ecosystem Tools — Stage 1**

| **Tool** | **Primary Use at Stage 1** | **Free? Available in M365?** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Microsoft Teams (Class Teams) | Create class environment; communicate with learners; share files and resources | Yes — M365 Education |
| Microsoft Outlook | Professional email; calendar management; meeting invites | Yes — M365 |
| Microsoft OneDrive | Cloud file storage; sharing resources; accessing files from any device | Yes — M365 |
| Microsoft Word | Creating accessible lesson resources, schemes of learning, and documentation | Yes — M365 |
| Microsoft PowerPoint | Presentations; visual resources for teaching and learning | Yes — M365 |
| Office Accessibility Checker | Checking any document or presentation for WCAG 2.2 AA compliance before sharing | Yes — built into all Office apps |
| Immersive Reader and Assistive Technology | Demonstrating inclusive reading support to all learners at induction, and regularly after. Promoting the use of assistive technology for learning. | Yes — built into Teams, Word, OneNote, Forms. AT often found in M365 and device systems. |
| SharePoint (view only) | Accessing Quality Hub, team sites, and shared college documents | Yes — M365 |

### **CPD Levers — Stage 1**

The following CPD mechanisms from the Weston College Quality Assurance and Improvement Manual 2025/26 are most relevant to supporting staff at Stage 1. Each is named as it appears in the handbook.

| **CPD Lever** | **How it supports Stage 1 development** | **When it happens** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| New Staff Induction (NSI) and Extended New Staff Induction | The four-day academic staff induction includes a dedicated Digital TLA session. The Extended NSI on Wednesdays in Term 1 includes Digital TLA sessions. The induction timetable includes Scavenger Hunt tasks using SharePoint, Teams, SMART Board, and Microsoft Forms as an early digital orientation. | Monthly throughout the year; August and September cohorts largest. Extended NSI: Wednesdays, Term 1 |
| COGs — Digital Pedagogy | Four times a year staff learn to upskill in different elements of Digital Pedagogy. The focus areas are: Digital Learning Environments; Accessibility and Inclusion; Digital TLA; Using AI to support learning; 21st Century Learning Design. | Four times a year |
| Developmental Observations | New staff to Weston College receive a developmental observation in addition to any learning walk. Staff who want further coaching on their TLA practice can self-refer to their TLAM. The observation focuses on the quality of teaching and learning, including the environment and resources available online. Digital Accessibility, Digital Environments, and Digital Inclusion can be a focus within the Dev Obs system. | Windows 1, 2, and 3 throughout the year |
| 1:1 Coaching with Digital Pedagogy Coach | Graeme Wright as Digital Pedagogy Coach provides individual coaching and mentoring to support improvement of Digital TLA practice across the college. Stage 1 staff benefit most from targeted, individual coaching conversations focused on building tool confidence and establishing digital habits. | On referral or self-referral throughout the year |
| Individual CPD | Jisc Building Digital Capability self-assessment and recommended learning pathways. ETF DTPF modules are the primary digital routes at this stage. All CPD is recorded on iTrent. | Throughout the year at own pace |
| Teach-Meet | Several Teach-Meet style sessions can be arranged to support group and team training with various foci: Teams Environments; Accessibility; Digital Inclusion; AI; Digital TLA. | Several themed sessions per term |

## **Stage 2A: Adopting (Developing)**

### **Pyramid Level: Digital Inclusion | ETF DTPF: Adopting, Developing | DigCompEdu: B1 Integrator**

Staff at Stage 2A have moved beyond basic tool use and are beginning to integrate digital purposefully into their teaching practice. They use Teams Assignments with more intention. They are aware of and beginning to apply the college AI policy. They are starting to see digital tools as a means of reducing their own workload and improving learner experience. The focus shifts from confidence to application.

### **General Digital Skills — Stage 2A**

| **Skill Area** | **What staff should be able to demonstrate** |
| --- | --- |
| Efficient M365 workflows | Use Outlook calendar for scheduling and sharing availability. Use 'Book with Me' or 'Bookings' for sharing meeting time availability. Use Teams channels to organise communication by topic. Filter, search, and retrieve emails efficiently. Use Copilot to interrogate emails and improve communication. Use OneNote or MS Loops for organised meeting notes. |
| Data handling and spreadsheets | Create and use Excel spreadsheets with basic formulae. Use filters and sorting. Understand GDPR in the context of learner data. Record and interpret basic datasets. Use Copilot to interrogate data. Navigate other platforms like Power BI to understand the context of learners and their learning needs. |
| Digital content creation | Create accessible presentations using correct heading structure and alt text. Create visually engaging content. Use Canva or equivalent for infographics and visual resources. Export accessible PDFs from Word. Create Digital Accessible Content by Design. |
| AI tool awareness | Understand what Copilot is, what it can and cannot do, and what the college AI policy says. Use AI tools to reduce admin workload where permitted, for example quiz generation, resource drafting, or meeting summaries. Always verify outputs. |
| Secure digital practice | Understand Caldicott principles for learner data if working in health-adjacent provision. Use GDPR-compliant file-sharing. Know the difference between college data storage and personal cloud services. |

### **Teaching Digital Skills — Stage 2A**

| **Skill Area** | **What staff should be able to demonstrate** |
| --- | --- |
| Teams Assignments with rubrics | Create Assignments with grading rubrics attached. Return work with written feedback via the Grades tab. Enable Teams Analytics and review basic engagement data for the class. |
| Formative assessment with Forms | Create Microsoft Forms quizzes for formative assessment. Embed them in Teams. Review results in real time to identify misconceptions. Use the Forms summary view to inform next lesson planning. |
| Accessible resource design | Apply WCAG 2.2 AA principles to all uploaded resources as a consistent professional habit, not an occasional check. Alt text on all images. Correct heading structure. Captions on all video. Colour contrast checked. |
| Inclusive tools for learners | Demonstrate Immersive Reader with translation enabled for ESOL learners. Enable Live Captions as a default in all Teams sessions. Know how to set up Dictation and Speech to Text for learners who need it. Introduce Read and Write Gold for learners with SpLD. |
| Hybrid readiness | Deliver an accessible, equivalent learning experience to learners attending remotely. Share slides and resources in the Teams session. Use the chat and reactions features inclusively. Know how to record a session for learners who miss it. |

### **Microsoft Ecosystem Tools — Stage 2A**

| **Tool** | **Primary Use at Stage 2A** | **Free? Available in M365?** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Teams Assignments and Rubrics | Structured digital assessment with grading criteria; feedback via Grades tab | Yes — M365 Education |
| Microsoft Forms | Formative quizzes; initial assessment; self-assessment; feedback collection | Yes — M365 |
| Teams Analytics | Class engagement data; assignment completion rates; communication patterns | Yes — M365 Education |
| Microsoft Sway | Visually engaging digital presentations and content pages for learners | Yes — M365 |
| Microsoft Stream | Recording and sharing lesson videos; captions generated automatically | Yes — M365 |
| Microsoft Copilot (where licensed) | Quiz and resource generation; meeting summaries; lesson plan drafting. Use only in accordance with the college AI policy and after completing Jisc AI Literacy Module 1. | Institutional licence — confirm status with IT |
| Read and Write Gold | Text to speech, word prediction, and study skills support for learners with SpLD | College licence — check availability with IT |
| Live Captions (Teams) | Real-time captions in 60+ languages as standard in all Teams sessions | Yes — M365. Requires IT to enable as default. |
| Canva for Education | Accessible visual content creation; infographics; presentations. Free for educators. | Yes — free educator account at canva.com/education |

### **CPD Levers — Stage 2A**

| **CPD Lever** | **How it supports Stage 2A development** | **When it happens** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Half-termly Teach Meets (Section and Area Level) | The primary half-termly CPD mechanism at area level. The QIP Tab 2 template explicitly links CPD and Teach Meet activity to QIP priorities and impact on TLA. Digital topics can be foregrounded here. The Digital Pedagogy Coach can present, model, or co-facilitate. | Half-termly, each curriculum area |
| Learning Walks and Developmental Observations | At Stage 2A, the Learning Walk focuses on whether digital tools are changing the learning experience, not just being present in the room. Are Assignments being used with rubrics? Is the Teams environment well structured? Is there evidence of accessible resources? | Three windows: September to November; January to March; April to June |
| Peer Reviews | Staff at Stage 2A benefit from reviewing peers who are stronger at digital TLA, and from being reviewed by colleagues exploring Stage 1. Reviews can focus on Teams environment quality, formative digital assessment, and inclusive tool use. All staff complete at least one peer review per year outside their own area. | Throughout the year; captured in CQRP windows |
| Jisc AI Literacy Module 1 (Foundation Awareness) | This is the prerequisite for any AI tool adoption into teaching. Free via Jisc institutional access. Covers what AI tools are, how they work, ethical use, and the limitations staff need to understand before advising learners. Must be completed before using Copilot or any AI tool in a teaching context. | Self-paced; complete before any AI tool adoption |
| Jisc Building Digital Capability (BDC) Self-Assessment | The Jisc Discovery Tool for staff provides a personal digital capability profile across six dimensions. At Stage 2A, staff revisit their profile to see progress and identify the next development priorities. Free for Jisc subscribing institutions. | On enrolment and annually; review mid-year |
| Curriculum Quality Review Panels (CQRPs) | CQRPs include a TLA Overview section covering Self, Peer, and QRA evidence. Digital TLA development is increasingly visible in this evidence. TLAMs can use the Digital Health Check data as a CPD impact measure in these panels. | Twice per year |

## **Stage 2B: Adopting (Embedding)**

### **Pyramid Level: Digital Inclusion | ETF DTPF: Adopting, Embedding | DigCompEdu: B2 Expert**

Stage 2B is where digital practice becomes genuinely embedded rather than occasionally applied. Staff at this level design learning sequences where digital tools purposefully change the experience for learners. Accessibility is a habit, not a check. They can articulate which EDS categories their learners are developing, they know how to run the Digital Health Check, and they are becoming a credible resource for colleagues. The Digital Lead role sits at Stage 2B moving towards Stage 3.

### **General Digital Skills — Stage 2B**

| **Skill Area** | **What staff should be able to demonstrate** |
| --- | --- |
| Advanced M365 workflows | Use Power Automate to automate a repetitive task such as notifications, data collection, or file organisation. Use Microsoft Lists to organise and track information. Use SharePoint to create structured team sites for curriculum resources. Build replicable workflows that colleagues can adopt. |
| Data analysis and reporting | Use Excel pivot tables and basic charts to analyse learner data. Use Power BI dashboards for programme performance data where available. Interpret Teams Assignment analytics to inform feedback and planning. Present data in accessible, meaningful formats for quality conversations. |
| AI tools in practice | Use Copilot (where licensed) to draft resources, generate quizzes, and summarise meeting notes. Always review and verify outputs. Understand when AI generated content requires disclosure. Model responsible AI use explicitly for learners. Use Copilot for data interrogation and content refinement as part of routine workflow. |
| Digital project coordination | Use Teams Planner or Microsoft Lists to manage a project or CPD activity. Share progress with colleagues digitally. Use SharePoint for collaborative document management rather than emailed attachments. |
| Digital professional presence | Maintain a professional digital profile on LinkedIn. Share good practice via college digital channels. Contribute to Teach Meets with digital examples. Understand the public digital footprint of a professional educator. |

### **Teaching Digital Skills — Stage 2B**

| **Skill Area** | **What staff should be able to demonstrate** |
| --- | --- |
| Digital curriculum design | Design lesson sequences where digital tools purposefully change the learning experience. The VLE is a live, learner-directed environment, not a file repository. Learners use digital tools in authentic vocational contexts rather than as isolated skills lessons. Every session demonstrates deliberate digital pedagogy choices. |
| Accessibility by design | Every resource created passes the Office Accessibility Checker before being shared — this is a professional habit, not an occasional compliance check. Captions on all video content. Alt text on all images. Correct heading structure in all documents. Digital content is designed for all learners from the outset, not retrofitted. |
| EDS mapping | Articulate which EDS categories learners are developing in their programme. Map digital hours to EDS categories in the Business Plan. Use DSF Tab 3 as a reference in curriculum planning. Contribute to the coaching conversation with the Digital Pedagogy Coach. |
| Formative assessment pipeline | Use the Forms to Excel to Copilot model or equivalent to aggregate formative assessment data and identify learner gaps systematically. Learner digital skills progress is tracked alongside subject progress. Assessment design actively builds learner digital capability. |
| Assistive technology for inclusion | Know what assistive technology is available to learners across the college. Introduce AT tools proactively to all learners rather than only those with a recorded need on their ILP. Advocate for AT resources where gaps exist. Demonstrate AT tools confidently in session. |
| Digital Health Check | Run the Teams Digital Health Check for all class Teams each half-term. Address any gaps. Share the Health Check results with the TLAM. Support colleagues who are at Stage 1 with their own Teams environment. |

### **Microsoft Ecosystem Tools — Stage 2B**

| **Tool** | **Primary Use at Stage 2B** | **Free? Available in M365?** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Microsoft Forms to Excel pipeline | Formative assessment data collection and aggregation. Results feed directly into Excel for analysis and gap identification. | Yes — M365 |
| Microsoft Lists | Organising project tasks, tracking CPD, managing curriculum information | Yes — M365 |
| SharePoint Sites | Creating structured team sites for curriculum resources accessible to learners and colleagues | Yes — M365 |
| Power Automate (basic flows) | Automating repetitive notifications, data collection from Forms, or file organisation tasks | Yes — M365 |
| Microsoft Power BI (basic) | Interpreting programme performance dashboards where available | Yes — M365 (view); Pro required for creation |
| Microsoft Flip | Short student video responses; peer feedback; formative assessment alternatives to written work | Yes — free, included in M365 Education |
| Microsoft Copilot (critical use) | Resource generation, quiz drafting, meeting summaries: always reviewed and verified. Modelled transparently for learners. Only after Jisc AI Literacy Module 1 is complete. | Institutional licence — confirm status with IT |
| Tobii Eye Gaze / Proloquo2Go / Widgit (awareness) | Understanding what AT tools are available for FIP and SEND learners. Not necessarily operating them, but knowing they exist and advocating for them. | College licences — check with SEND team and IT |
| Microsoft Reading Coach | Free AI-powered reading fluency tool embedded in Teams; particularly valuable for ESOL and literacy development | Yes — free, built into M365 Education |

### **CPD Levers — Stage 2B**

| **CPD Lever** | **How it supports Stage 2B development** | **When it happens** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Digital Lead Induction (to be produced) | Staff confirmed as Digital Leads for their area receive a structured induction covering the Digital Health Check process, EDS mapping, and their role in supporting colleagues. The Digital Lead sits at Stage 2B and moves towards Stage 3. | On confirmation as Digital Lead; support ongoing from Digital Pedagogy Coach |
| Quality Review Activity (QRA) | QRAs provide Ofsted-style scrutiny of the quality of education, including the learning environment and digital resources available. At Stage 2B, staff should be able to demonstrate accessible resources, a well-structured Teams environment, and learner evidence of digital skills development. | At-risk or volume basis throughout the year; one week's notice given |
| Peer Reviews (cross-area) | At Stage 2B, peer reviews with colleagues from different areas are particularly valuable for cross-pollinating digital practice. The Digital Pedagogy Coach can broker peer review pairings. Reviews focus on curriculum design, inclusion, and assessment. | Throughout the year; captured in CQRP windows |
| Jisc FE Accessibility Training Matrix Phase 2 | A structured self-assessment and training pathway for staff digital accessibility knowledge. Maps to roles across the college. Free via Jisc institutional access. Particularly important for staff in areas with SEND or ESOL learners. | Self-paced; recommended before Digital Lead confirmation |
| Teach Meets with Digital Showcase | Stage 2B staff present good practice at half-termly Teach Meets. This is where the Forms to Excel to Copilot model, accessible resource design, and AT good practice get shared across teams. Teach Meets are the primary vehicle for spreading Stage 2B practice. | Half-termly |
| Programme Performance Reviews (PPRs) | PPRs are held three times per year and focus on learner outcomes. Digital skills tracking data, Teams Assignment analytics, and Jisc Discovery Tool learner profiles all become PPR evidence at Stage 2B. The Digital Pedagogy Coach can provide data in advance. | Three times per year |

## **Stage 3: Leading**

### **Pyramid Level: Digital Innovation | ETF DTPF: Leading | DigCompEdu: C1 to C2 Leader to Pioneer**

Stage 3 staff are not just expert digital practitioners. They are agents of change. They coach and mentor colleagues, design digital-first curriculum, evaluate EdTech critically, and lead their institution's thinking on AI, accessibility, and digital innovation. Their practice does not stay in their classroom. It spreads. The Digital Pedagogy Coach role sits at Stage 3. The best Digital Leads will move here.

### **General Digital Skills — Stage 3**

| **Skill Area** | **What staff should be able to demonstrate** |
| --- | --- |
| Workflow automation at scale | Build multi-step Power Automate flows that connect Forms, Lists, Teams notifications, and SharePoint. Create replicable models that other staff can adopt. The Forms to Excel to Copilot pipeline is an example of Stage 3 thinking made accessible. Contribute automation models to the area Digital Lead network. |
| Strategic data use | Use Power BI or Excel dashboards to inform strategic decisions about curriculum, CPD, or inclusion. Present data to TLAMs and SLT in a compelling, accessible format. Connect learner data to EDS progression evidence. Contribute meaningful digital skills data to the SAR and QIP cycle. |
| AI orchestration | Go beyond using AI tools to shaping how they are used institutionally. Understand AI policies, advise on ethical frameworks, model transparent AI use, and challenge colleagues' over-reliance on AI outputs without critical verification. Lead the college's thinking on responsible AI adoption. |
| Digital leadership communication | Influence strategy through digital channels. Contribute to strategic documents, Quality Improvement Plans, and Business Plans with digital skills evidence. Use SharePoint and Teams to make good practice visible and accessible to all staff across the college. |
| Open educational resources and knowledge sharing | Create resources that others can use and adapt. Publish good practice digitally. Contribute to external networks including Jisc communities and ETF sector groups. Represent Weston College in regional and national digital communities. |

### **Teaching Digital Skills — Stage 3**

| **Skill Area** | **What staff should be able to demonstrate** |
| --- | --- |
| Digital-first curriculum design | Design curriculum where the VLE is a live, learner-directed environment from day one. Learners are co-designers of digital resources. Authentic vocational tasks use digital tools as the default, not the exception. Digital design decisions are justified pedagogically, not just technologically. |
| Critical EdTech evaluation | Evaluate new digital tools against pedagogical rationale, GDPR compliance, accessibility, sustainability, and cost before adoption. Challenge tools that add complexity without improving learner outcomes. Advocate for tools that demonstrably reduce barriers. Use the Jisc Digital Elevation Tool findings to inform evaluation decisions. |
| Coaching and mentoring colleagues | Coach Stage 1 and Stage 2 staff on digital pedagogy in a way that builds their confidence rather than creating dependency. Use the Digital Learning Pyramid and the Jisc BDC profile to structure development conversations. Support the TLAM in identifying and addressing digital CPD needs across the team. |
| Institutional AI leadership | Lead the team's thinking on AI in teaching and assessment. Develop and communicate clear guidance for learners on academic integrity and AI use. Model transparent AI practice. Advocate for Jisc AI Literacy Module 1 as a prerequisite for all staff before using AI tools in a teaching context. |
| Digital inclusion advocacy | Champion assistive technology investment and training across the college. Advocate for WCAG 2.2 AA as a non-negotiable standard in all digital resource creation. Represent learners' digital access needs in quality conversations. Connect Learning Without Barriers to the QFT and UDL frameworks explicitly. |
| Digital Champions development | Identify and develop Digital Champions among learners in the curriculum area. Support DCI learners in their peer mentoring role. Connect Champions across areas. Contribute to the Digital Champions programme at a strategic level. |

