# **Section 7: The Digital Development Strategy**

This section brings together the what, why, and how of supporting digital skills and digital learning across Weston College. It is designed to make visible the connection between the Digital Learning Pyramid, the Teacher Digital Skills framework, QFT and UDL principles, and the quality processes that already exist. The strategy is not a new set of obligations. It is a way of naming what good practice already looks like and building more of it, systematically.

## **What I am trying to achieve**

The purpose of digital pedagogy coaching at Weston College is to create the conditions where every learner, regardless of their programme, their background, their ability, or their access to technology, can develop the digital skills they need for learning, for work, and for life.

That purpose cannot be achieved by one person. It requires a whole-college approach where TLAMs, coaches, Heads of Area, Digital Leads, and teaching staff are all working with a shared vocabulary, a shared framework, and shared evidence of what good looks like. That is what the Digital Learning Pyramid, the Teacher Digital Skills framework, and the Digital Skills Framework together are designed to create.

## **Why digital skills matter for quality, QFT, UDL, and 21CLD**

| **Framework** | **The connection to digital skills development** |
| --- | --- |
| Quality First Teaching (QFT) | QFT requires high quality, inclusive, differentiated teaching without separate intervention. Digital tools, when designed in from the start, are the practical infrastructure of QFT. Accessible resources, Live Captions as a default, Immersive Reader available to everyone: these are QFT in action in a digital age. |
| Universal Design for Learning (UDL) | UDL's three principles, multiple means of representation, action and expression, and engagement, are most richly realised when digital tools are embedded in teaching rather than added as afterthoughts. Every inclusive digital tool in this document addresses at least one UDL principle directly. |
| 21st Century Learning Design (21CLD) | 21CLD identifies knowledge building, real-world problem solving, skilled communication, collaboration, ICT for learning, and self-regulation as the activities most likely to develop future-ready learners. All six are most powerfully realised through authentic, embedded digital tasks in vocational contexts. |
| Ofsted EIF Report Card | The renewed EIF (November 2025) grades Inclusion as a standalone area for the first time. Digital inclusion, accessible resources, assistive technology provision, and systematic digital skills tracking are all directly evidenceable against the Inclusion and Curriculum and Teaching Report Card dimensions. The coaching strategy produces this evidence as a byproduct of doing good work. |

## **How the quality team can support this**

The Digital Pedagogy Coach role sits within the Quality team and reports through the quality structure. This is a strength, not a limitation. It means that digital skills development is embedded in the quality cycle rather than running parallel to it. The connection between the coaching work and the inspection evidence is direct.

| **Quality Process** | **How digital skills development connects** |
| --- | --- |
| Learning Walks and Developmental Observations | Digital dimensions are visible in every Learning Walk: is the Teams environment well structured? Are resources accessible? Are digital tools purposefully changing the learning experience? The Digital Pedagogy Coach can co-design the digital observation criteria with TLAMs. |
| Curriculum Quality Review Panels (CQRPs) | CQRPs include a TLA Overview covering Self, Peer, and QRA evidence. Digital skills coaching records, RAG ratings, and action plans feed this evidence directly. The Digital Coaching Platform is designed specifically to surface this data for quality panels. |
| Programme Performance Reviews (PPRs) | Learner digital skills progression, Jisc Discovery Tool pre and post data, Teams Assignment analytics, and Century diagnostic data all become PPR evidence. The coaching strategy embeds digital evidence into the performance review cycle. |
| Quality Improvement Plans (QIPs) | Each area's QIP Tab 2 links CPD and Teach Meet activity to TLA impact. Digital skills development actions sit naturally in this structure. The Digital Pedagogy Coach supports TLAMs in articulating digital AFIs and progress measures in their QIPs. |
| Self-Assessment Report (SAR) | The Digital Pedagogy Coach leads the digital skills dimension of the annual SAR, drawing on the macro view of the Digital Coaching Platform, the Jisc DEI Survey results, and the collective coaching evidence from across all 30 areas. |

| **The invisible impact** Much of the impact of strong digital pedagogy is soft and almost invisible in outcome data. A learner who can navigate a government portal independently. A student who self-corrects an AI output rather than submitting it unchecked. A staff member who runs the Accessibility Checker as a reflex rather than an obligation. These are not measurable in exam results. They are visible in the quality of learner experience, in the confidence of staff, and in the readiness of the college to meet the digital expectations of employers, inspectors, and the wider world. That is the case for this work. It is worth making explicitly. |
| --- |

## **The road ahead**

The immediate priorities for the digital strategy in 2026/27 are clear. Every area has a named, trained Digital Lead. Every class Team meets the Digital Health Check standard. Digital hours are mapped to EDS categories in every Business Plan. The Jisc Discovery Tool is deployed in September 2026. All resources are Accessible by Design by Christmas 2026. The Digital Coaching Platform is built and in use by TLAMs and the Coach from the start of the autumn term.

The longer term ambition is a college where digital skills development is as consistently tracked, evidenced, and named as English and Maths progression. Where every Head of Area can answer the question: what digital skills have your learners developed this year, and how do you know? Where Weston College is recognised regionally and nationally as a college that takes digital inclusion seriously and can prove it.

That is where this framework, this strategy, and this document are pointing.

 

*Sources and further reading are provided in the Digital Skills Leadership Toolkit (digital-skills-leadership-toolkit-v4.html) and the Digital Skills Framework document (digital-skills-framework.docx). All external sources were verified in March 2026.*

# **Section 8: Collated Learning Pathways — Beginner to Innovator**

Three complete learning pathways, one for each audience, taking learners from absolute beginner to innovator. Each pathway is structured in four stages: Beginner (no assumed prior experience), Developing (building confidence and applying skills), Embedding (consistent, purposeful practice), and Innovating (leading, shaping, and extending). Courses are drawn from LinkedIn Learning, Microsoft Learn, Jisc, ETF, and other verified free providers. All URLs verified March 2026.

| **How to use these pathways** These pathways are not rigid sequences. They are signposted routes. A learner may enter at any stage. The Jisc Building Digital Capability Discovery Tool (jisc.potential.ly/signin) and the Century Tech Initial Assessment provide the personal baseline that determines where on a pathway each individual begins. Pathway progress is reviewed at mid-year using the same tools. The Digital Coaching Platform RAG rating maps to the four pathway stages: Red = Beginner, Amber (lower) = Developing, Amber (upper) = Embedding, Green = Innovating. |
| --- |

## **Pathway 1: Students — Learn, Work, Life**

This pathway supports learners across all curriculum areas in developing the digital skills they need for their studies, for future employment, and for independent living. It maps to the DfE Essential Digital Skills (EDS) framework across five categories: Using Devices and Handling Information; Creating and Editing; Communicating; Transacting; and Being Safe and Responsible Online. It is designed for delivery alongside Jisc Discovery Tool assessment (New Students question set in September, Current Students mid-year, Employability Skills end of year) and Century Tech L1 and E3 diagnostic themes.

| **Starting point: Jisc Discovery Tool for students** All learners complete the Jisc Discovery Tool New Students question set in Weeks 1 to 2 of induction. The personalised report generates recommended resources across the six digital capability elements. The Digital Pedagogy Coach provides the institution link via jisc.potential.ly/signin. Results inform the TLAM and inform coaching conversations. Learners revisit at mid-year using the Current Students set and at end of year using Employability Skills. |
| --- |

